      "certainty": "Observed",
      "headline": "Special Weather Statement issued November 23 at 1:16AM HST by NWS Honolulu HI",
      "description": "Kilauea eruption episode 57 began at 12:15 AM HST this morning.\nLava fountain heights within Halemaumau Crater are currently\nestimated at a few hundred feet high. The latest radar imagery has\ndetected small volcanic particles as high as 14,500 feet.\nLight northerly surface winds near the volcano will push low level\nparticles towards the south of the crater. Winds aloft above the\nlow level boundary layer will blow from the northeast direction\npushing tephra towards the southwest direction.\n\nWe will continue to coordinate with USGS scientists on local ashfall\nimpacts as this latest Kilauea eruption and volcanic plume continues\nto evolve over time.\n\nAvoid excessive exposure to ash which is an eye and respiratory\nirritant. Those with respiratory sensitivities should take extra\nprecaution to minimize exposure.",
